course: dictionary
lesson: 3689
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them all, and the context tells you which meaning the writer means. The word flare can be a noun or a verb, and it has several meanings. «Do not touch strange metal objects, drums or flares on the beach; tell a grown-up or lifeguard.» Here a flare is a very bright light used as a signal for help.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «The Sun is not calm: it has dark sunspots, flares and a wind of particles.» Here flares are huge bursts of energy from the Sun, not lights on a boat. Ask yourself what the text is about. Is it safety at sea, space science, or a story about anger? The topic is your first clue.
explain: Flare can also be a doing word, a verb. «After a match adrenaline stays high and anger can flare before the thinking part of the brain catches up.» Here to flare means to start suddenly and get stronger. «A barbecue stays hot for hours, and fuel must never be squirted onto it because it flares up.» Flare up means catching fire suddenly, which is dangerous.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to check it. In «steroid creams for flare-ups», a flare-up is a sudden time when an illness gets worse, and the sentence still makes sense. When a word has several meanings, test each one and keep the meaning that fits the context.
fact: Many English words have more than one meaning, and this is normal.
fact: «Flare» can be a noun, meaning a very bright light or flame used as a signal for help, or a verb, meaning to burn suddenly brightly.
fact: «Flare up» means something bad starts suddenly or gets worse, like anger, a fire or an illness.
fact: To «flare» can also mean to get wider at the bottom, like a skirt, trousers or nostrils.
fact: Never touch a flare you find on a beach; tell a grown-up or lifeguard straight away.
exercise: «Do not touch strange metal objects, drums or flares on the beach.» What is a flare in this sentence? | A kind of sea bird | A big wave that arrives suddenly | A sandy hill made by the wind | *A very bright light or flame used as a signal for help
exercise: «The Sun is not calm: it has dark sunspots and flares.» What are solar flares? | Stars that fall to the ground | Clouds that hide the Earth | *Huge bursts of energy from the Sun | Cold winds from the north
exercise: «After a match, anger can flare before the thinking part of the brain catches up.» What does flare mean here? | To make a bright signal for help | *To start suddenly and get stronger | To cool down slowly | To get wider at the bottom
test: «Fuel must never be squirted onto a barbecue because it flares up.» What does flares up mean here? | Gets shorter | *Catches fire suddenly | Turns cold and wet | Falls asleep
test: «Mia's flared trousers were wider at the bottom.» What does flared mean? | Got shorter at the top | Were very bright | *Got wider at the bottom | Were made of metal
exam: In a story about a boat in trouble, the captain fires a flare into the sky. What is the flare for? | To hide the boat from other ships | To light a birthday cake | *To signal for help | To keep the boat warm
